<p class="nx-meta"><a href="/notes/">Guides</a><span>Jobs</span><span>7 min read</span></p>
<h1>AI is hitting the first rung of the career ladder first</h1>
<p class="nx-dek">There is still no sign of economy-wide job loss from AI. There is a clear sign of fewer young people being hired into the jobs most exposed to it.</p>
<div class="nx-byline"><span>By <b>Twinthos Research</b></span><span>Published <b><time datetime="2026-10-08">8 October 2026</time></b></span><span>Updated <b><time datetime="2026-10-13">13 October 2026</time></b></span><span>4 sources, all linked</span></div>
</div></header>
<figure class="nx-fig"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/assets/notes-img/ladder-800.webp 800w, /assets/notes-img/ladder-1600.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 1160px"><img src="/assets/notes-img/ladder-1600.jpg" srcset="/assets/notes-img/ladder-800.jpg 800w, /assets/notes-img/ladder-1600.jpg 1600w" sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 1160px" width="1600" height="900" alt="A tall plain wooden ladder leaning against a bare concrete wall, the lowest rungs in window light and the upper rungs in shadow" fetchpriority="high"></picture><figcaption>Illustration generated for this guide. It is not a photograph of a real place.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="wrap nx-layout">
<aside class="nx-toc" aria-label="Contents"><p>In this guide</p><ol><li><a href="#what-the-research-found">What the research found</a></li><li><a href="#how-it-happens-fewer-hires-not-more-firings">How it happens: fewer hires, not more firings</a></li><li><a href="#the-odd-part-ai-helps-beginners-the-most">The odd part: AI helps beginners the most</a></li><li><a href="#what-the-data-does-not-show">What the data does not show</a></li><li><a href="#what-employers-expect-next">What employers expect next</a></li><li><a href="#what-this-means-for-a-small-employer">What this means for a small employer</a></li></ol></aside>
<div class="nx-article">
<section class="nx-take" aria-label="Key takeaways"><h2>The short version</h2><ul><li>Stanford's August 2026 update finds <strong>no evidence of widespread, economy-wide job displacement</strong> from generative AI.</li><li>But employment of workers aged 22 to 25 in AI-exposed jobs now stands <strong>19% below</strong> where it would be had it matched their less-exposed peers. Experienced workers show no comparable gap.</li><li>The mechanism is <strong>reduced hiring</strong>, not more layoffs. Pay has not fallen.</li><li>The authors call these early, descriptive indicators, not proof of cause. Read them as a warning, not a verdict.</li></ul></section>

<p>Two things are true at once. Generative AI has not caused broad job losses. And a specific group, young workers in the occupations AI can do most of, is finding it harder to get hired. The best evidence comes from a Stanford Digital Economy Lab study that has been updated twice, most recently in August 2026.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></p>
<h2 id="what-the-research-found">What the research found</h2>
<p>Researchers Erik Brynjolfsson, Bharat Chandar and Ruyu Chen used payroll data from ADP covering millions of US workers through June 2026. They report six facts.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></p>
<ul class="nx-stats"><li><strong>19%</strong><span>gap for workers aged 22 to 25 in AI-exposed jobs, against less-exposed peers</span></li><li><strong>0</strong><span>evidence of widespread, economy-wide job displacement</span></li><li><strong>~20%</strong><span>fall in employment of software developers aged 22 to 25, from the 2022 peak to September 2025</span></li></ul>
<figure class="nx-chart nxc-bars" id="c-gap" role="group" aria-labelledby="c-gap-t"><figcaption class="nx-chart-head"><span class="nx-chart-kicker">Chart</span><strong id="c-gap-t">Employment of workers aged 22 to 25 in AI-exposed jobs, relative to less-exposed peers</strong></figcaption><ul class="nx-hbars"><li class="nx-row dim"><span class="nx-lab">Earlier estimate (data to 2025)</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:64.0%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">16% below</span></li><li class="nx-row warn"><span class="nx-lab">August 2026 update (data to June 2026)</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:76.0%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">19% below</span></li></ul><p class="nx-chart-note">Experienced workers in the same occupations show no comparable gap. The authors note the divergence has widened steadily since they first documented it in August 2025.</p><p class="nx-chart-src">Sources: Stanford Digital Economy Lab, <a href="https://digitaleconomy.stanford.edu/publications/canaries-in-the-coal-mine/" rel="noopener">Canaries in the Coal Mine?</a> (revised 12 August 2026); Stanford AI Index 2026, Chapter 4 (earlier estimate).</p></figure>
<p>Stanford's 2026 AI Index reports the software side of the same pattern. Employment among the youngest workers has declined since 2022 even as headcount for older age groups keeps growing, and by September 2025 employment for software developers aged 22 to 25 had fallen close to 20% from its 2022 peak.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></sup></p>
<h2 id="how-it-happens-fewer-hires-not-more-firings">How it happens: fewer hires, not more firings</h2>
<p>The paper's fourth and fifth facts explain the mechanism. The divergence operates primarily through reduced hiring of young workers rather than increased separations. And declines are concentrated in occupations where AI use primarily substitutes for human tasks. Where AI usage primarily complements workers, employment is flat or rising, especially for experienced workers.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></p>
<blockquote class="nx-pull"><p>Nobody is being sacked. A door is closing quietly at the bottom of the ladder.</p></blockquote>
<p>That distinction matters. Layoffs are visible and measured. A role that never gets advertised leaves no trace in the unemployment figures, which is partly why the headline numbers look calm while the age breakdown does not. The authors also find that adjustment is happening through employment rather than base pay.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></p>
<h2 id="the-odd-part-ai-helps-beginners-the-most">The odd part: AI helps beginners the most</h2>
<p>Stanford's Index collects studies of AI productivity effects. Customer support agents using an AI assistant resolved 14% to 15% more issues per hour. Software developers using GitHub Copilot completed 26% more pull requests. Marketing teams using AI for ad creation saw a 50% increase in output per worker. A consistent finding across several of the studies is that less experienced workers tended to benefit the most.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></sup></p>
<figure class="nx-chart nxc-bars" id="c-prod" role="group" aria-labelledby="c-prod-t"><figcaption class="nx-chart-head"><span class="nx-chart-kicker">Chart</span><strong id="c-prod-t">Productivity gains reported in studies cited by the AI Index</strong></figcaption><ul class="nx-hbars"><li class="nx-row hi"><span class="nx-lab">Marketing output per worker</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:90.9%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">+50%</span></li><li class="nx-row "><span class="nx-lab">Software developer pull requests</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:47.3%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">+26%</span></li><li class="nx-row "><span class="nx-lab">Customer support issues resolved per hour</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:26.4%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">+14 to 15%</span></li></ul><p class="nx-chart-note">Gains are largest in structured, measurable work. For tasks needing deeper reasoning, some studies found little benefit or a slowdown. METR's early-2025 trial of experienced developers found a 19% slowdown, but METR has since said those results no longer reflect current tools.</p><p class="nx-chart-src">Source: Stanford HAI, <a href="https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/ai_index_report_2026_chapter_4_economy.pdf" rel="noopener">AI Index 2026, Chapter 4</a>, summarising Brynjolfsson et al. (2025), Cui et al. (2025), Ju and Aral (2025).</p></figure>
<p>So the tool helps newcomers the most, and newcomers are the ones being hired less. The two findings are not a contradiction. They are a description of a market where an employer can get a beginner's output from software, and so hires fewer beginners. The Index adds a warning that heavy AI reliance may carry long-term learning penalties that slow skill development over time.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></sup></p>
<h2 id="what-the-data-does-not-show">What the data does not show</h2>
<ul><li><strong>It does not prove cause.</strong> The authors call their results "early, descriptive indicators, canaries in the coal mine, rather than causal estimates."<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></li>
<li><strong>The result survives some obvious objections.</strong> It persists when excluding technology firms and computer occupations, and when controlling for exposure to interest-rate rises and for remote work.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></li>
<li><strong>It weakens in others.</strong> The patterns attenuate when controlling for education, show some trends that predate generative AI, and are stronger in the payroll sample than in national survey benchmarks.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></li>
<li><strong>It is US data.</strong> The payroll sample covers US workers. Do not assume the same size of effect elsewhere.</li></ul>
<h2 id="what-employers-expect-next">What employers expect next</h2>
<figure class="nx-chart nxc-stack" id="c-exp" role="group" aria-labelledby="c-exp-t"><figcaption class="nx-chart-head"><span class="nx-chart-kicker">Chart</span><strong id="c-exp-t">Expected change in workforce size from AI over the next year, all surveyed organisations</strong></figcaption><div class="nx-stack"><span class="nx-seg dim" style="--w:43%"><b>43%</b></span><span class="nx-seg warn" style="--w:32%"><b>32%</b></span><span class="nx-seg hi" style="--w:13%"><b>13%</b></span><span class="nx-seg " style="--w:12%"><b>12%</b></span></div><ul class="nx-stackleg"><li class="nx-key dim"><i></i>Little or no change <b>43%</b></li><li class="nx-key warn"><i></i>Decrease <b>32%</b></li><li class="nx-key hi"><i></i>Increase <b>13%</b></li><li class="nx-key "><i></i>Do not know <b>12%</b></li></ul><p class="nx-chart-note">Anticipated reductions are highest in service operations, supply chain and software engineering. Across nearly all functions, anticipated decreases outpace those already observed.</p><p class="nx-chart-src">Source: Stanford HAI, <a href="https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-index/2026-ai-index-report/economy" rel="noopener">AI Index 2026, economy takeaways</a>, citing the McKinsey &amp; Company Survey, 2025.</p></figure>
<h2 id="what-this-means-for-a-small-employer">What this means for a small employer</h2>
<aside class="nx-callout reading"><span class="nx-callout-l">Our reading</span><p>The risk for a business is quiet. If you stop hiring juniors because software does the first-draft work, you also stop training the people who would be your experienced staff in five years. The practical response is to be deliberate about which tasks go to software and which stay with people who are learning.</p></aside>
<ol><li><strong>List the junior tasks.</strong> What did your last junior hire actually spend the week on? Data entry, scheduling, chasing and first drafts are the tasks software handles best.</li>
<li><strong>Move those tasks to a system.</strong> Give them a defined process with a named person who checks the result.</li>
<li><strong>Rebuild the junior role around judgment.</strong> The new hire reviews the system's output, handles exceptions, and talks to customers. That is the apprenticeship, and it is a better one.</li></ol>

<section><h2>Common questions</h2><div class="nx-faq"><details><summary>Is AI taking entry-level jobs?</summary><div class="a"><p>The strongest evidence says AI is reducing hiring of young workers in the most AI-exposed occupations, not causing economy-wide job losses. Stanford's August 2026 update finds employment of 22 to 25 year olds in those jobs is 19% below where it would be, with no comparable gap for experienced workers. The authors describe the findings as descriptive, not proof of cause.</p></div></details><details><summary>Which entry-level jobs are most affected?</summary><div class="a"><p>Occupations where AI use primarily substitutes for human tasks. The Stanford AI Index highlights software developers and customer service agents, where employment among workers aged 22 to 25 has declined since 2022 while older age groups have grown.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are layoffs rising because of AI?</summary><div class="a"><p>Not according to this research. The paper finds the divergence operates primarily through reduced hiring of young workers rather than increased separations, and finds no evidence of widespread, economy-wide job displacement.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does AI help junior workers?</summary><div class="a"><p>In productivity studies cited by Stanford, less experienced workers tended to benefit the most from AI tools. That is one reason employers can get beginner-level output without a beginner, which is the pressure on entry-level hiring.</p></div></details></div></section>
<section class="nx-src"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li id="src-1"><a href="https://digitaleconomy.stanford.edu/publications/canaries-in-the-coal-mine/" rel="noopener">Canaries in the Coal Mine? Six Facts about the Recent Employment Effects of Artificial Intelligence (revised 12 August 2026)</a> <span>Brynjolfsson, Chandar and Chen, Stanford Digital Economy Lab</span><span>ADP administrative payroll data covering millions of US workers through June 2026.</span></li><li id="src-2"><a href="https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/ai_index_report_2026_chapter_4_economy.pdf" rel="noopener">The 2026 AI Index Report, Chapter 4: Economy (PDF)</a> <span>Stanford Institute for Human-Centered AI</span><span>Software developer employment, productivity studies, learning-penalty note.</span></li><li id="src-3"><a href="https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-index/2026-ai-index-report/economy" rel="noopener">The 2026 AI Index Report, Economy: top takeaways</a> <span>Stanford Institute for Human-Centered AI</span><span>Employer workforce expectations, McKinsey &amp; Company Survey, 2025.</span></li><li id="src-4"><a href="https://metr.org/blog/2026-02-24-uplift-update/" rel="noopener">Update on METR&#x27;s developer productivity study, 24 February 2026</a> <span>METR</span><span>States that the early-2025 slowdown result no longer reflects current tools.</span></li></ol></section>
<div class="nx-log"><b>Corrections and updates</b><p>13 October 2026. Corrected a number. The earlier version said software developer employment aged 22 to 25 fell nearly 20&nbsp;percent "since 2024". Stanford's summary page uses that wording, but the chapter text gives the figure as close to 20% from the 2022 peak by September 2025, which is what this guide now says.</p><p>13 October 2026. Updated to Stanford Digital Economy Lab's August 2026 revision (19% gap, data to June 2026), and added the mechanism, caveats and employer-expectation data.</p></div>
</div></div>
</article>
